Linear IgA Bullous Dermatosis (Mild Localised)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Small tense vesicles, often drug-induced (e.g., Vancomycin), localized distribution

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-026
Reason for Visit: Acute eruption of fluid-filled vesicles requiring immediate exclusion of Stevens-Johnson Syndrome or systemic anaphylaxis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate a sudden onset blistering rash following recent oral antibiotic use. Immediate clinical examination was strictly necessary to rule out early Stevens-Johnson Syndrome or a severe anaphylactic reaction to prevent potentially fatal mucosal and epidermal sloughing prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I took the new antibiotics yesterday, and tonight my arms are covered in these little blisters. I'm terrified my skin is going to peel off.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a scattered, mild blistering eruption commencing approximately 6 hours ago. Symptoms began with localized mild pruritus on the bilateral forearms, evolving rapidly into discrete, fluid-filled blisters. Patient was recently prescribed an oral antibiotic for a minor soft tissue infection. The sudden presentation caused significant distress regarding a severe, life-threatening drug allergy. Denies lip swelling, dysphagia, dyspnea, or ocular involvement.
Functional Status: Independent
Social/Safety Concerns: Low risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/82 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.0 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, hemodynamically stable, visibly anxious but no signs of systemic toxicity or respiratory distress.
Physical Exam: Dermatological exam reveals small tense vesicles, often drug-induced, localized distribution to the extensor surfaces of the forearms. Lesions are 2-4mm, arranged in discrete annular clusters ("string of pearls" sign). The vesicles contain clear serous fluid. Nikolsky sign is strictly negative. Oropharyngeal, conjunctival, and genital mucosa are completely clear and unaffected. Cardiovascular and respiratory exams are normal.
Environmental Observations: Safe environment, lighting adequate for skin examination.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient recently commenced an oral antibiotic course 48 hours ago.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Linear IgA Bullous Dermatosis (Mild Localised) - Status: Acute Onset
Differentials:

Stevens-Johnson Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of mucosal involvement, negative Nikolsky sign, and lack of systemic prodromal symptoms (high fever, severe malaise).

Acute Anaphylaxis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal respiratory effort, absence of wheeze, and no angioedema or urticarial wheals.

Bullous Impetigo - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Non-purulent fluid, intact tense vesicles, and absence of characteristic honey-colored crusting.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute blistering eruption shortly after commencing a new medication, establishing a high clinical suspicion for a severe cutaneous adverse reaction such as Stevens-Johnson Syndrome. Triage correctly mandated urgent evaluation to ensure airway stability and prevent widespread epidermal loss. Clinical examination definitively established the absence of mucosal involvement and a negative Nikolsky sign, confirming a localized, non-necrotizing bullous reaction. This immediate bedside assessment safely prevented an unnecessary emergency department admission for suspected SJS, allowing the offending drug to be ceased and outpatient therapy to be initiated safely.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed immediate cessation of the offending oral antibiotic. Administered potent topical corticosteroid ointment to the affected areas to reduce blistering.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply a cool, damp cloth to the blistered areas for 10 minutes prior to applying the steroid cream. By cooling the skin first, you will rapidly constrict local blood vessels and decrease the intense itch, naturally reducing your urge to scratch and pop the blisters.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 24-48 hours to prescribe an alternative antibiotic and arrange dermatology referral for biopsy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you develop any sores inside your mouth, if your eyes become red and painful, or if you experience difficulty breathing.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal skin biopsy and immunofluorescence; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: This is a specific immune reaction where antibodies attack a layer of the skin, causing blisters. It is triggered by the medication and will begin resolving once the drug is fully cleared from your system.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if the skin begins to peel away in large sheets when rubbed, or if you develop a high fever.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The medication caused a mild allergic blistering response, but it is not the severe, life-threatening skin shedding disease. Stopping the drug stops the reaction.
Actionable Steps: Do not take any more of the current antibiotic. Do not pop the blisters, as intact skin prevents secondary bacterial infection.

SMS INFORMATION

